Installing portconf
===================

Gentoo (recommended)
--------------------
Install from the ::stuff overlay <https://github.com/istitov/stuff>:

    # Add the overlay (one-time, choose either):
    #   eselect repository add stuff git https://github.com/istitov/stuff
    # or write /etc/portage/repos.conf/stuff.conf with sync-uri set to
    # https://github.com/istitov/stuff
    emaint sync -r stuff
    emerge app-portage/portconf

This is the only supported installation method for production Gentoo systems.
The ebuild handles the correct install paths and dependencies automatically.


From source (manual)
--------------------
Requirements: autoconf >= 2.69, automake >= 1.13, bash, sed.

    autoreconf -i          # only needed from a git checkout
    ./configure
    make
    doas make install      # installs to /usr/local by default

To install system-wide (matching the ebuild layout):

    ./configure --prefix=/usr --sysconfdir=/etc
    make
    doas make install

Optional configure switches:

    --disable-bash-completion   skip installing completion/portconf
                                (default: install to $datadir/bash-completion/completions)
    --disable-zsh-completion    skip installing completion/_portconf
                                (default: install to $datadir/zsh/site-functions)


Runtime dependencies
--------------------
portconf requires the following tools at runtime:

    app-portage/eix
    app-portage/portage-utils   (qlist, qatom)
    sys-apps/portage            (emerge, eselect)

The -rc / --regen-cache option (strongly recommended) also requires eix-update.


Action modes
------------
portconf is a dry-run by default and writes no persistent state.  Use --ask
to apply changes interactively, or --force for a non-interactive automation
run.  The historical -y / --yes option remains an alias for --force.


Running the test suite
----------------------
Unit tests (no runtime tools required beyond bats):

    make check                  # or: bats tests/unit/

Integration tests exercise not_found, invalid_uses, and invalid_uses_make
against real Portage tooling.  The following must be on PATH:

    app-portage/eix
    app-portage/portage-utils   (qatom)
    dev-libs/tre                (agrep)

Run with:

    make check-integration      # or: bats tests/integration/

Integration tests read /etc/portage directly via eix -Ttc and are therefore
host-sensitive: they expect a working, populated Portage tree.

Smoke tests run the BUILT src/portconf binary end-to-end against the host's
real Portage tree (eselect profile list, /etc/portage/make.profile, eix).
They cover read-only flags plus every mutating dispatch arm against throwaway
roots in the default dry-run mode — enough to catch substitution failures,
profile-tree layout drift, action-policy bypasses, and eix/eselect
output-format regressions without mutating the host's /etc/portage or
/var/lib/portconf.  Requires bats, eselect, eix.

    make check-smoke            # or: bats tests/smoke/


Configuration
-------------
A sample configuration file is installed to /etc/portconf.conf.
Uncomment and adjust the settings as needed.  The defaults are safe.


Environment overrides (for tests, chroots, ROOT prefixes)
---------------------------------------------------------
portconf honors the following environment variables; each falls back to
the canonical modern Gentoo layout when unset.  Override only if you
know what you are doing — pointing them at the wrong location can sweep
or modify real system state.

    PORT_ETC      path to the config dir       (default: /etc/portage)
    BRDIR         portconf's tarball backup dir (default: /var/lib/portconf)
    PKGDB         installed-package db root    (default: /var/db/pkg)
    WORLD         path to the world file       (default: /var/lib/portage/world)
    DEP_PATH      eix dep-cache root           (default: /var/cache/edb/dep)

These are primarily intended for the bats integration test harness to
sandbox each function.  The legacy /etc/make.conf and /etc/make.profile
fallbacks are honored only when PORT_ETC is at its default location
(so a sandboxed PORT_ETC never accidentally pulls in real /etc/ paths).
